WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.140
Are we sitting comfortably?

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:02.090
Yeah, we're good.

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:02.620
We are.

00:00:02.620 --> 00:00:03.540
How do you sit?

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:04.190
Like sit back?

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:05.940
Sit where you want, just sit!

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:07.860
I set him a challenge.

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:09.600
Long and short of it, I set him a
challenge.

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:11.600
We've done lots of different traditional
events

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:13.380
to raise money for the Brain Tumour
Charity.

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:16.940
Being a chef, it's difficult to train for
marathons.

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:18.680
They haven't gone down well.

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:22.560
And I've kind of sneaked out of all those
big family training sessions.

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:23.640
This is my marathon.

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:30.020
This is not a traditional warm chicken,
two veg charity dinner.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:31.500
You're at Tottenham Court Road, so you're

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:33.080
right in the middle of central London.

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:35.840
And there's this amazing gold building in
front of you.

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:37.760
We looked at sort of fine dining.

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:38.520
We looked at music.

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:41.700
And we looked at how we could combine them
all.

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:45.460
Visual, sound, light, the biggest stage in
front of you.

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:46.600
We're going to have Bicep.

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:48.000
They're sound tracking the whole evening.

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:50.520
You feel like each sense is heightened.

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:51.720
We're going to start with smell.

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:53.100
Turn the room into a garden.

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:54.980
Put this amazing scent around the room.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:56.520
So when you sit there, you can smell.

00:00:57.040 --> 00:00:57.920
Like, you're in that moment.

00:00:57.940 --> 00:00:58.540
You're in the garden.

00:00:58.540 --> 00:00:59.740
And next one will be sight.

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:00.820
All really colourful.

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:02.180
Bright, bold, it's exciting.

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:02.900
It's very pretty.

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:03.440
[unclear]

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:04.520
Don't reveal too many of the surprises.

00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:06.260
[unclear]

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:07.660
Buy the tickets.

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:11.480
Both of our parents died from brain
tumours.

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:14.160
Both just had headaches, which, for some
people, that's normal.

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:17.380
Our mum was 42 when she died.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:18.120
And we were children.

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:20.320
And our dad was 59.

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:22.060
And we were sort of in our mid-20s.

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:23.480
I've got a headache for a week, when he
got

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:25.200
checked out, he went, oh, you have a brain
tumour.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:26.780
You've got six weeks left and that's it.

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:29.640
We were unlucky that both of them had the
worst form.

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:31.480
Like the doctor said to us, you basically
won

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:33.580
the lottery, but the worst lottery in the
world.

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:37.480
We want to raise awareness just because it
is so random.

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:39.620
Life expectancy is tiny.

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:40.680
Post-diagnosis.

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:43.240
My mum was four months, and dad was six
weeks.

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:44.640
There's not much time.

00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:45.860
There isn't a cure.

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:48.520
It's crazy how fast it can happen to
people.

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:50.500
And obviously, people never think about
it.

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:51.740
Well, it's not linked to anything.

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:53.000
No one knows how it happens.

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:55.700
There's been no improvement in, really

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:58.060
in survival rates in the last 40, 50
years.

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:01.220
And it does disproportionately affect
people under the age of 40.

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:02.480
You know, that's our age.

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:04.120
Our age of people, really.

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:07.220
You know, over the years, we've really
wrapped up

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:10.120
that fundraising tally to the Brain Tumour
Charity.

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:13.020
But I think we want to see if we can do

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:15.200
something bigger in memory of our parents.

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:16.680
My dad always wanted to help people.

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:18.140
He always saw the good in everybody.

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:19.340
He created a legacy.

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:23.140
So it's now our chance to give that back,
to make an impact.

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:24.860
And if we bring the right people together,

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:27.320
that extra 1% makes a big difference.

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:30.080
And I think this is the kind of legacy
that we want to create,

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:32.820
because we believe this idea is going to
have the biggest impact.

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:37.180
The one thing that I would say is we have
held nothing back.

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:41.600
It's our first year doing it, and we
really want it to be exceptional.

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:43.960
I want people to share memories and share
moments,

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:48.840
because even if a course gives them a
memory, that's amazing.

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:50.480
Your brain creates your personality.

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:53.640
It's a celebration of all the memories
that make you you.

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:56.880
An experience, something that is unique.

00:02:57.520 --> 00:02:58.220
And raising money.

00:02:58.400 --> 00:02:59.050
Yes.
